# mytheus-platform / env/prod.db.temporal.env - the TEMPORAL database credential.
# Copy to env/prod.db.temporal.env and fill. NEVER commit the filled file.
#
# Read by the `temporal` service only. Temporal's auto-setup image creates and owns its own
# schemas (temporal, temporal_visibility) and therefore connects as the postgres superuser,
# so this value is the same secret as POSTGRES_PASSWORD in env/prod.env. It lives in its own
# file for two reasons.
#
# First, the image expects the name POSTGRES_PWD, not POSTGRES_PASSWORD, so it cannot simply
# read prod.env. Handing it prod.env to pick up one variable would also hand it every Clerk,
# Stripe and LiveKit credential in there, which it has no business holding - the same
# least-privilege argument that gives web, relay and migrate separate files.
#
# Second, this used to be `POSTGRES_PWD: ${POSTGRES_PASSWORD}`. Interpolation resolves from
# the shell or the project .env before compose parses anything and can never read an
# env_file, so an interpolated value must be handed to whatever runs the deploy. Dokploy
# stores that in its database and serves it back in plaintext to any API-key holder. A file
# keeps the superuser password on the host and out of that blast radius.
#
# Set this to the SAME value as POSTGRES_PASSWORD in env/prod.env. A mismatch surfaces as
# temporal failing its schema setup on boot rather than as anything subtler.
POSTGRES_PWD=CHANGE_ME_same_as_POSTGRES_PASSWORD
